// Series 7 Daily Ten question bank. In each entry, c[0] is the correct answer; the app shuffles choices on screen.
window.S7_QUESTIONS = [{"id":1,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"A registered representative emails the same market commentary to 40 retail customers during one week. Under FINRA rules, this is classified as:","c":["A retail communication","Correspondence","An institutional communication","A research report"],"e":"A written or electronic communication made available to more than 25 retail investors within any 30-calendar-day period is a retail communication. Correspondence goes to 25 or fewer retail investors in that window."},
{"id":2,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"Which statement about correspondence is accurate under FINRA's communications rule?","c":["It may be reviewed after it is sent, under the firm's written supervisory procedures","A principal must approve each piece before it is sent","It must be filed with FINRA within 10 business days of first use","It may only be sent to institutional investors"],"e":"Correspondence (25 or fewer retail investors within 30 days) does not need principal approval before use. Firms may review it after distribution, following their written supervisory procedures."},
{"id":3,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"A broker-dealer in its first year of FINRA membership plans to run a newspaper advertisement. When must the ad be filed with FINRA?","c":["At least 10 business days before first use","Within 10 business days after first use","Only if FINRA requests it","At least 30 calendar days before first use"],"e":"For one year after becoming a member, a firm must file retail communications used in public media with FINRA at least 10 business days before first use."},
{"id":4,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"An established member firm creates a retail communication promoting a mutual fund. It contains no rankings the firm created itself. The filing requirement is:","c":["File with FINRA within 10 business days of first use","File with FINRA at least 10 business days before first use","No filing, since a principal approved it","File with the SEC before first use"],"e":"Retail communications about registered investment companies are generally filed with FINRA within 10 business days of first use. Pre-filing applies to special cases such as self-created rankings."},
{"id":5,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"An options advertisement will reach people who have not yet received the Options Disclosure Document. The advertisement may:","c":["Give a general description of the options being offered","Recommend a specific options strategy on a named stock","Show the past performance of the firm's options recommendations","Project an annualized rate of return for covered writing"],"e":"Options communications used before delivery of the ODD must be limited to general descriptions. They cannot include recommendations or past or projected performance figures."},
{"id":6,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"A representative based in New York wants to cold call a prospect who lives in California. What is the earliest time the call may be placed?","c":["8:00 a.m. Pacific time","8:00 a.m. Eastern time","9:00 a.m. Pacific time","Any time, as long as the prospect is not on a do-not-call list"],"e":"Telemarketing calls are permitted between 8:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m. in the local time of the person being called."},
{"id":7,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"During a cold call, a prospect who is not on the national registry says, \"Do not call me again.\" The representative must:","c":["Have the person placed on the firm's do-not-call list","Wait 30 days before calling again","Mail a written disclosure before the next call","Do nothing, because the person is not on the national registry"],"e":"Each firm must keep its own do-not-call list and honor any request to be added to it, whether or not the person is on the national registry."},
{"id":8,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"Which statement about institutional communications is accurate?","c":["The firm may not treat them as institutional if it expects them to reach retail investors","A principal must approve each one in writing before it is used","Each one must be filed with FINRA at least 10 days before use","They may be sent to retail investors if a disclaimer is attached"],"e":"Institutional communications need no pre-approval or filing, but the firm must have written supervisory procedures and cannot treat a piece as institutional if it has reason to believe it will reach retail investors."},
{"id":9,"f":1,"t":"Communications","q":"For how long must a member firm keep copies of its retail communications?","c":["Three years from the date of last use","One year from the date of first use","Six years from the date of last use","For the life of the firm"],"e":"Retail and institutional communications must be retained for three years from the date of last use, with the first two years in an easily accessible place."},
{"id":10,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"During the cooling-off period for a new stock offering, a registered representative may:","c":["Accept a nonbinding indication of interest","Accept a deposit to reserve shares","Confirm a sale that becomes final on the effective date","Send the firm's research report recommending the issue"],"e":"Between filing and the effective date, firms may distribute the preliminary prospectus and take nonbinding indications of interest. No sales may be made and no money may be accepted."},
{"id":11,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"Which individual qualifies as an accredited investor under Regulation D?","c":["A person with a net worth of $1.5 million, not counting a primary residence","A married couple with joint income of $250,000 in each of the last two years","A person who earned $180,000 in each of the last two years","A person with $1.2 million of net worth, $900,000 of which is home equity"],"e":"An individual is accredited with net worth above $1 million excluding the primary residence, or income above $200,000 ($300,000 jointly) in each of the last two years with the same expected this year."},
{"id":12,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"Under Rule 506(b) of Regulation D, a private placement may be sold to:","c":["Unlimited accredited investors plus up to 35 non-accredited investors","No more than 35 investors in total, of any type","Accredited investors only, with no limit on their number","Any number of investors, whether accredited or not"],"e":"Rule 506(b) allows unlimited accredited investors plus up to 35 non-accredited purchasers, and it does not permit general solicitation."},
{"id":13,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"A control person wants to sell stock under Rule 144. The company has 4,000,000 shares outstanding. Weekly trading volume for the last four weeks was 38,000, 42,000, 46,000 and 50,000 shares. What is the most the person may sell in the next 90 days?","c":["44,000 shares","40,000 shares","50,000 shares","176,000 shares"],"e":"The limit is the greater of 1% of outstanding shares (40,000) or the average weekly volume over the prior four weeks (176,000 / 4 = 44,000)."},
{"id":14,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"An investor buys restricted stock of a company that files reports with the SEC. Under Rule 144, how long must the investor hold the shares before selling them publicly?","c":["Six months","Ninety days","One year","Two years"],"e":"Restricted securities of an SEC reporting company must be held for six months. For a non-reporting company, the holding period is one year."},
{"id":15,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"Rule 144A allows:","c":["Resale of restricted securities to qualified institutional buyers","Sale of unregistered securities to any accredited individual","Public resale of control stock with no volume limit","Intrastate offerings without federal registration"],"e":"Rule 144A creates a market for restricted securities among qualified institutional buyers (QIBs), generally institutions with at least $100 million invested in securities."},
{"id":16,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"A resident of Ohio buys shares in an intrastate offering made under Rule 147. When may the investor first resell the shares to a resident of another state?","c":["Six months after the issuer sold the shares to the investor","Immediately, if the buyer is accredited","Thirty days after the offering ends","One year after the offering ends"],"e":"Under Rule 147, resales during the six months after the issuer's sale to the purchaser may be made only to residents of the same state."},
{"id":17,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"A company completes an IPO and its shares begin trading on the NYSE. For how long must dealers deliver a prospectus to buyers in the secondary market?","c":["25 days","40 days","90 days","There is no requirement"],"e":"For an IPO of a security that will be listed on an exchange or Nasdaq, the aftermarket prospectus delivery period is 25 days. It is 90 days for an IPO of an unlisted security."},
{"id":18,"f":1,"t":"New issues","q":"Under FINRA's rule on new issues, which person is generally prohibited from buying shares of an equity IPO?","c":["A registered representative of a member firm that is not in the underwriting syndicate","A public school teacher","A restaurant owner who is a long-time customer of the underwriter","A retired accountant with no ties to the securities industry"],"e":"Rule 5130 bars restricted persons, including broker-dealers and their associated persons, from buying new equity issues, even when their own firm is not part of the offering."},
{"id":19,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Under the Customer Identification Program rules, a firm must collect each of the following from an individual before opening an account EXCEPT:","c":["Net worth","Date of birth","Residential address","Taxpayer identification number"],"e":"The CIP minimum is name, date of birth, address and an identification number. Financial details such as net worth are gathered for suitability, not for identity verification."},
{"id":20,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A new retail customer refuses to name a trusted contact person. The firm:","c":["May open the account, because it made a reasonable effort to obtain the information","May not open the account","May open the account only for unsolicited trades","Must name the registered representative as the trusted contact"],"e":"FINRA requires a reasonable effort to obtain a trusted contact (age 18 or older) for non-institutional accounts. A customer may decline and the account can still be opened."},
{"id":21,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Two friends own an account as tenants in common, with 60% and 40% interests. The 60% owner dies. What happens to that interest?","c":["It passes to the deceased owner's estate","It passes to the surviving tenant","It is split equally between the survivor and the estate","It is liquidated and paid to the survivor"],"e":"In a tenants-in-common account, a deceased owner's share goes to that person's estate. In joint tenancy with right of survivorship, it would pass to the survivor."},
{"id":22,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Which of the following is permitted in an UTMA account?","c":["A gift of securities from a grandparent who is not the custodian","Two custodians sharing responsibility for one minor","Buying securities on margin","Registering the account under the custodian's Social Security number"],"e":"Anyone may give irrevocable gifts to an UTMA account. Each account has one custodian and one minor, uses the minor's Social Security number, and cannot trade on margin."},
{"id":23,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A customer tells her representative, \"Buy 500 shares of XYZ today, whenever you think the price is right.\" This order:","c":["Is not discretionary, because only time and price were left open","Is discretionary and needs written trading authorization","May not be accepted by the representative","Is discretionary and needs a principal's approval before entry"],"e":"Discretion over only time and price is not treated as a discretionary order. The customer specified the asset, the action (buy) and the amount."},
{"id":24,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Before a representative may make discretionary trades in a customer's account, the firm must have:","c":["Written authorization from the customer and written acceptance by the firm","The customer's verbal approval, noted on each order ticket","A signed margin agreement and a loan consent form","Approval of the account by FINRA's district office"],"e":"Discretionary authority requires prior written authorization from the customer and written acceptance by the firm. A principal must also review discretionary activity frequently."},
{"id":25,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Which sequence correctly describes opening an options account?","c":["Deliver the ODD, obtain ROP approval, enter the first trade, then receive the signed options agreement within 15 days","Enter the first trade, deliver the ODD, obtain ROP approval, then receive the signed agreement","Receive the signed options agreement, enter the first trade, deliver the ODD, then obtain ROP approval","Obtain ROP approval, enter the first trade, then deliver the ODD within 15 days"],"e":"The ODD must be delivered at or before account approval. A Registered Options Principal approves the account before the first trade, and the customer returns the signed options agreement within 15 days of approval."},
{"id":26,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"An options account was approved 20 days ago and the customer has not returned the signed options agreement. The customer may now enter:","c":["Closing transactions only","Opening and closing transactions","Opening purchases only","No orders of any kind"],"e":"If the signed options agreement is not returned within 15 days of account approval, the account is limited to closing transactions until the agreement is received."},
{"id":27,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Which document is NOT required to open a margin account?","c":["Loan consent agreement","Credit agreement","Hypothecation agreement","New account form"],"e":"The credit agreement and hypothecation agreement are required. The loan consent, which lets the firm lend the customer's securities to others, is optional."},
{"id":28,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A registered representative employed by one member firm wants to open a brokerage account at a different member firm. What is required?","c":["Prior written consent from the representative's employer","Nothing, as long as the account is a cash account","Approval from FINRA","Notice to the employer within 30 days after the account is opened"],"e":"An associated person must get the employer's prior written consent and must notify the executing firm, in writing, of the association before the account is opened."},
{"id":29,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Regulation S-P requires broker-dealers to:","c":["Give customers a privacy notice and a way to opt out of some information sharing","Report cash deposits above $10,000 to FinCEN within 15 days","Verify each customer's identity before opening an account","Send account statements to customers at least quarterly"],"e":"Regulation S-P covers the privacy of customer information. Firms must deliver privacy notices and provide an opt-out for sharing with nonaffiliated third parties."},
{"id":30,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A representative recommends a series of trades. Each trade is suitable on its own, but together they are excessive given the customer's profile. Which suitability obligation is violated?","c":["Quantitative suitability","Reasonable-basis suitability","Customer-specific suitability","Institutional suitability"],"e":"Quantitative suitability looks at whether a series of recommended transactions is excessive when viewed together. Excessive trading of this kind is also called churning."},
{"id":31,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"Regulation Best Interest applies when a broker-dealer:","c":["Recommends a securities transaction or investment strategy to a retail customer","Executes an unsolicited order for an institutional customer","Provides fee-based advice as a registered investment adviser","Holds customer funds in a cash account"],"e":"Reg BI is triggered by a recommendation to a retail customer of a securities transaction, an investment strategy involving securities, or an account type."},
{"id":32,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A customer deposits $6,000 in cash in the morning and another $5,500 in cash that afternoon. The firm must:","c":["File a Currency Transaction Report for the day's total","Do nothing, because neither deposit exceeds $10,000","Refuse the second deposit and return the cash","Freeze the account for 90 days"],"e":"Cash transactions by one person are aggregated for the day. A total above $10,000 requires a Currency Transaction Report to FinCEN."},
{"id":33,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"After a firm files a Suspicious Activity Report about a customer's transactions, the firm:","c":["Must not tell the customer that the report was filed","Must notify the customer within 30 days","Must close the customer's account","Must send a copy of the report to the customer"],"e":"SAR filings are confidential. Tipping off the subject of the report is prohibited."},
{"id":34,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A firm learns that the owner of an individual account has died. What should the firm do first?","c":["Cancel open orders, mark the account deceased and freeze the assets","Liquidate all positions and hold the cash for the heirs","Transfer the assets to the customer's spouse right away","Continue following standing instructions until the estate is settled"],"e":"On notice of death, the firm cancels open orders, marks the account deceased and freezes the assets until it receives documents such as a death certificate and letters testamentary."},
{"id":35,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A customer gave her daughter a durable power of attorney over her brokerage account. The customer is later declared mentally incompetent. The daughter's authority:","c":["Continues until the customer dies","Ended when the customer was declared incompetent","Continues after the customer's death until the estate is settled","Must be renewed by a court every year"],"e":"A durable power of attorney stays in force if the grantor becomes incapacitated. Every power of attorney ends when the grantor dies."},
{"id":36,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A firm reasonably believes a 72-year-old customer is being financially exploited after she asks to wire most of her account to an unfamiliar third party. Under FINRA rules, the firm may:","c":["Place a temporary hold on the wire and notify her trusted contact","Sell all positions and hold the proceeds in cash","Close the account and mail her a check for the balance","Do nothing other than follow the instruction as given"],"e":"FINRA Rule 2165 lets a firm place a temporary hold on disbursements or transactions when it reasonably believes a specified adult (age 65 or older, or impaired) is being exploited."},
{"id":37,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A customer moves her account to a new broker-dealer through ACATS. After the carrying firm validates the transfer instruction, it must complete the transfer within:","c":["Three business days","One business day","Seven calendar days","Ten business days"],"e":"The carrying firm has one business day to validate the transfer instruction, then three business days after validation to complete the transfer."},
{"id":38,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A broker-dealer fails. A customer's account held $150,000 of securities and $300,000 of cash. How much does SIPC cover?","c":["$400,000","$450,000","$500,000","$250,000"],"e":"SIPC covers up to $500,000 per separate customer, of which no more than $250,000 may be cash. Here that is $150,000 of securities plus $250,000 of cash."},
{"id":39,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"To open a margin account for a corporation, a firm needs:","c":["A corporate resolution, plus the charter or bylaws permitting margin","Only the signature of the corporation's president","A partnership agreement signed by all officers","Written approval from the corporation's transfer agent"],"e":"The corporate resolution names who may trade for the corporation. For margin or options, the firm also needs the charter or bylaws to confirm the activity is permitted."},
{"id":40,"f":2,"t":"Customer accounts","q":"A firm solicits a new customer to buy a penny stock. Before the first trade, the firm must:","c":["Deliver a risk disclosure document and get a signed suitability statement","Obtain written approval from the stock's issuer","Require the customer to open a margin account first","Wait 30 days after the account is opened before trading"],"e":"For customers who are not established customers, the penny stock rules require a risk disclosure document and a written suitability determination that the customer signs."},
{"id":41,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"A shareholder owns 500 shares of a company that uses cumulative voting. Four directors are up for election. What is the largest number of votes she can cast for a single candidate?","c":["2,000","500","125","4,000"],"e":"Cumulative voting gives shares times seats (500 x 4 = 2,000 votes), and the holder may cast them all for one candidate. This helps minority shareholders."},
{"id":42,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"A preemptive right gives a common stockholder the right to:","c":["Buy newly issued shares first, to keep the same ownership percentage","Receive dividends before preferred stockholders are paid","Vote on the amount of each quarterly dividend","Sell shares back to the company at par value on demand"],"e":"Preemptive rights let existing stockholders buy newly issued shares in proportion to their holdings, so their ownership is not diluted."},
{"id":43,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"A company has 6% cumulative preferred stock with a $100 par value. It paid $2 per share in year one and nothing in year two. To pay a common dividend in year three, how much must it first pay per preferred share?","c":["$16","$6","$10","$12"],"e":"Arrears are $4 from year one and $6 from year two. Adding the current year's $6 gives $16 before common stockholders can be paid."},
{"id":44,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"A convertible preferred stock can be exchanged for 4 shares of common stock. The preferred is trading at $88. What is the parity price of the common stock?","c":["$22","$84","$92","$352"],"e":"Parity is the preferred price divided by the conversion ratio: $88 / 4 = $22."},
{"id":45,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"An investor who owns an American Depositary Receipt:","c":["Receives dividends in U.S. dollars but still bears currency risk","Has no currency risk because the ADR trades in U.S. dollars","Must convert the ADR into foreign shares to receive dividends","Owns a debt obligation of a U.S. bank"],"e":"ADRs trade and pay dividends in dollars, but the dividend is declared in the foreign currency and the underlying share price reflects exchange rates, so currency risk remains."},
{"id":46,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"Which statement about warrants is accurate?","c":["They are long-term and usually issued with an exercise price above the stock's current market price","They are short-term and usually exercisable below the stock's current market price","They carry voting rights until they are exercised","They pay dividends at the same rate as the common stock"],"e":"Warrants are long-lived and typically issued as a sweetener with an exercise price above the market. Rights are short-term and exercisable below the market."},
{"id":47,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"An investor owns 200 shares of a stock trading at $60. The company declares a 3-for-2 split. After the split the investor owns:","c":["300 shares at about $40","300 shares at about $60","400 shares at about $30","133 shares at about $90"],"e":"A 3-for-2 split gives 3 shares for every 2 held (200 x 3/2 = 300). The price adjusts so total value is unchanged: $12,000 / 300 = $40."},
{"id":48,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"A company will pay a dividend to holders of record on Thursday, June 12. What is the last day an investor can buy the stock regular way and still receive the dividend?","c":["Wednesday, June 11","Thursday, June 12","Tuesday, June 10","Friday, June 13"],"e":"With T+1 settlement the ex-dividend date is the record date itself. A buyer must purchase at least one business day before the record date to settle in time."},
{"id":49,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"A stock trades at $40 and pays a quarterly dividend of $0.50 per share. What is its current yield?","c":["5.0%","1.25%","2.0%","8.0%"],"e":"Current yield is the annual dividend divided by the market price: ($0.50 x 4) / $40 = 5%."},
{"id":50,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"Which statement about real estate investment trusts (REITs) is accurate?","c":["They pass income through to shareholders but do not pass through losses","They pass both income and losses through to shareholders","They must distribute at least 50% of taxable income to avoid corporate tax","They are redeemable at net asset value"],"e":"A REIT that distributes at least 90% of its taxable income avoids tax on what it distributes. Unlike a limited partnership, it cannot pass losses through to investors."},
{"id":51,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"A corporation is authorized to issue 10,000,000 shares. It has issued 6,000,000 and holds 500,000 as treasury stock. How many shares are outstanding?","c":["5,500,000","6,000,000","9,500,000","4,000,000"],"e":"Outstanding shares equal issued shares minus treasury stock: 6,000,000 - 500,000 = 5,500,000. Treasury stock has no voting rights and receives no dividends."},
{"id":52,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"In a corporate liquidation, which claim is paid after general creditors but before preferred stockholders?","c":["Subordinated debenture holders","Common stockholders","Secured bondholders","Unpaid wages and taxes"],"e":"The order is secured creditors, then wages, taxes and general creditors (including debentures), then subordinated debt, then preferred stock, and common stock last."},
{"id":53,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"Holders of participating preferred stock:","c":["May receive dividends above the stated rate when the company does well","Vote for the board of directors","Can exchange their shares for common stock at a fixed ratio","Receive all skipped dividends before common dividends are paid"],"e":"Participating preferred can share in extra dividends beyond its fixed rate. Conversion describes convertible preferred, and catching up on skipped dividends describes cumulative preferred."},
{"id":54,"f":3,"t":"Equities","q":"Which type of preferred stock would most likely have the most stable market price as interest rates change?","c":["Adjustable-rate preferred","Cumulative preferred","Callable preferred","Straight (fixed-rate) preferred"],"e":"The dividend on adjustable-rate preferred resets with a benchmark rate, so its price does not need to move much when interest rates change."},
{"id":55,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A bond is trading at 108 and is callable at par. Which of its yields is the lowest?","c":["Yield to call","Yield to maturity","Current yield","Nominal yield"],"e":"For a premium bond the order from highest to lowest is nominal yield, current yield, yield to maturity, then yield to call, because the premium is lost over a shorter period if the bond is called."},
{"id":56,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A 6% corporate bond is trading at 80. What is its current yield?","c":["7.5%","6.0%","4.8%","8.0%"],"e":"Current yield is annual interest divided by market price: $60 / $800 = 7.5%."},
{"id":57,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"If market interest rates rise, which bond's price is likely to fall by the largest percentage?","c":["A 25-year zero-coupon bond","A 25-year bond with an 8% coupon","A 2-year note with a 3% coupon","A 6-month Treasury bill"],"e":"Price sensitivity increases with longer maturity and lower coupon. A long-term zero-coupon bond has the highest duration of the group."},
{"id":58,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A convertible bond has a conversion price of $40 and is trading at 110. What is the parity price of the common stock?","c":["$44","$40","$36.36","$27.50"],"e":"The conversion ratio is $1,000 / $40 = 25 shares. Parity is the bond's price divided by the ratio: $1,100 / 25 = $44."},
{"id":59,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A $1,000 par convertible bond has a conversion price of $25. Into how many shares can each bond be converted?","c":["40","25","4","250"],"e":"Conversion ratio equals par value divided by conversion price: $1,000 / $25 = 40 shares."},
{"id":60,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"An airline finances new aircraft by issuing bonds secured by the planes themselves. These bonds are:","c":["Equipment trust certificates","Collateral trust bonds","Debentures","Income bonds"],"e":"Equipment trust certificates are secured by specific equipment such as aircraft, railcars or trucks. Collateral trust bonds are secured by securities the issuer owns."},
{"id":61,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A debenture is backed by:","c":["The issuer's general credit only","A lien on the issuer's real estate","Securities held by a trustee","A guarantee from the federal government"],"e":"A debenture is an unsecured bond backed only by the issuer's promise to pay."},
{"id":62,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"An issuer is most likely to call its bonds when:","c":["Interest rates have fallen below the bonds' coupon rate","Interest rates have risen above the bonds' coupon rate","The bonds are trading at a deep discount","The issuer's credit rating has been lowered"],"e":"Issuers call bonds to refinance at lower rates. This leaves investors reinvesting at lower yields, which is why callable bonds usually offer a higher coupon."},
{"id":63,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"Compared with a coupon-paying bond of the same maturity, a zero-coupon bond held to maturity avoids:","c":["Reinvestment risk","Interest rate risk","Credit risk","Purchasing power risk"],"e":"A zero pays no periodic interest, so there are no coupons to reinvest at uncertain rates. Its price is more sensitive to rate changes, not less."},
{"id":64,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A corporate bond pays interest on January 1 and July 1. It is bought on Monday, March 9, for regular-way settlement. How many days of accrued interest does the buyer owe the seller?","c":["69","68","70","67"],"e":"Corporate bonds use 30-day months, and interest accrues up to but not including settlement (Tuesday, March 10): 30 for January, 30 for February and 9 for March."},
{"id":65,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"Which S&P rating is the lowest that is still considered investment grade?","c":["BBB","BB","A","B"],"e":"BBB (Baa from Moody's) is the lowest investment-grade category. BB and below are speculative, also called high yield."},
{"id":66,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"Commercial paper is exempt from SEC registration when its maturity does not exceed:","c":["270 days","90 days","180 days","One year"],"e":"Commercial paper is short-term unsecured corporate debt, usually issued at a discount. It is exempt from registration with a maturity of 270 days or less."},
{"id":67,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"Income (adjustment) bonds:","c":["Pay interest only if the issuer earns enough to cover it","Are a good choice for investors who need steady income","Are secured by the issuer's equipment","Must be repaid before the issuer's secured debt"],"e":"Income bonds are usually issued in a reorganization. Interest is paid only when earnings allow, so they are not suitable for investors who depend on income."},
{"id":68,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"An inverted yield curve shows that:","c":["Short-term rates are higher than long-term rates","Long-term rates are higher than short-term rates","Rates are the same at every maturity","Corporate yields are lower than Treasury yields"],"e":"In an inverted curve, short maturities yield more than long maturities. It often appears when the Fed has tightened credit and can come before a slowdown."},
{"id":69,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A corporate bond is quoted at 98 5/8. What is its dollar price per $1,000 of par?","c":["$986.25","$985.80","$980.63","$998.58"],"e":"Corporate bonds are quoted as a percentage of par in eighths. 98 5/8 = 98.625% of $1,000 = $986.25."},
{"id":70,"f":3,"t":"Corporate debt","q":"A bond's yield rises from 4.25% to 4.90%. The yield has increased by:","c":["65 basis points","6.5 basis points","0.65 basis points","650 basis points"],"e":"One basis point is 0.01%. The change is 0.65%, which equals 65 basis points."},
{"id":71,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"Treasury bills are:","c":["Issued at a discount and redeemed at face value","Issued at par with semiannual interest payments","Issued with maturities of 2 to 10 years","Callable by the Treasury at any time"],"e":"T-bills have maturities of one year or less and pay no coupon. The investor's return is the difference between the discounted purchase price and face value."},
{"id":72,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"A Treasury note is quoted at 101-08. What is its dollar price per $1,000 of par?","c":["$1,012.50","$1,010.80","$1,018.00","$1,001.08"],"e":"Treasury notes and bonds are quoted in 32nds. 101-08 means 101 and 8/32, or 101.25% of $1,000 = $1,012.50."},
{"id":73,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"During a period of inflation, a holder of Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS) will see:","c":["Principal adjusted upward, which raises each interest payment","The coupon rate reset higher every six months","No change until maturity, when an inflation bonus is paid","Principal adjusted downward to offset higher interest"],"e":"TIPS have a fixed coupon rate applied to a principal amount that is adjusted for changes in the CPI, so the dollar interest payments rise with inflation."},
{"id":74,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"Treasury STRIPS are best described as:","c":["Zero-coupon securities created from Treasury notes and bonds","Treasury bonds that pay interest every month","Mortgage-backed securities guaranteed by the Treasury","Short-term Treasury debt sold only to banks"],"e":"STRIPS are Treasury securities whose interest and principal payments have been separated and sold individually at a discount, with no periodic interest."},
{"id":75,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"Interest earned on U.S. Treasury notes is:","c":["Subject to federal income tax and exempt from state and local income tax","Exempt from federal income tax and subject to state income tax","Exempt from all income taxes","Subject to federal, state and local income taxes"],"e":"Interest on direct Treasury obligations is taxed federally but is exempt from state and local income taxes."},
{"id":76,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"Which security is backed by the full faith and credit of the U.S. government?","c":["GNMA pass-through certificates","FNMA mortgage-backed securities","FHLMC participation certificates","Federal Home Loan Bank notes"],"e":"Ginnie Mae is a government agency and its securities carry a direct government guarantee. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac securities do not."},
{"id":77,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"An investor owns GNMA pass-through certificates. If mortgage rates fall sharply, her main concern is:","c":["Prepayment risk","Credit risk","Extension risk","Liquidity risk"],"e":"When rates fall, homeowners refinance and principal is returned sooner than expected. The investor must then reinvest at lower rates."},
{"id":78,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"In a collateralized mortgage obligation, which tranche absorbs the most prepayment and extension risk?","c":["Companion tranche","Planned amortization class (PAC) tranche","Targeted amortization class (TAC) tranche","The earliest sequential-pay tranche"],"e":"Companion (support) tranches absorb prepayment variability to protect PAC and TAC tranches, so their average life is the least predictable."},
{"id":79,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"At a Treasury auction, noncompetitive bids are:","c":["Filled first, at the yield set by the competitive bidding","Filled only after every competitive bid is filled","Filled at whatever yield the bidder names","Not accepted from individual investors"],"e":"Noncompetitive bidders agree to accept the yield determined at the auction and are guaranteed to be filled. Competitive bidders name a yield and may not be filled."},
{"id":80,"f":3,"t":"Government debt","q":"Interest from which of these agency securities is exempt from state and local income tax?","c":["Federal Farm Credit Bank bonds","GNMA certificates","FNMA mortgage-backed securities","FHLMC mortgage-backed securities"],"e":"Interest on Federal Farm Credit Bank and Federal Home Loan Bank securities is exempt from state and local tax. Interest on mortgage-backed agency securities is taxable at every level."},
{"id":81,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A general obligation bond issued by a city is backed by:","c":["The city's full faith, credit and taxing power","Revenue from a specific project","Lease payments from a corporation","A guarantee from the federal government"],"e":"GO bonds are backed by the issuer's taxing power. For local governments the main source is ad valorem (property) taxes."},
{"id":82,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"Which statement about municipal revenue bonds is accurate?","c":["They are paid from user fees and are not subject to debt limits","They need voter approval before they can be issued","They are backed by the issuer's property taxes","They are normally sold through competitive bidding"],"e":"Revenue bonds are paid from the income of the facility they finance. They do not count against debt limits, need no voter approval, and are usually sold by negotiation."},
{"id":83,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A toll road has gross revenues of $5 million, operating and maintenance costs of $2 million, and annual debt service of $1.5 million. What is its debt service coverage ratio under a net revenue pledge?","c":["2 to 1","3.33 to 1","1.33 to 1","0.5 to 1"],"e":"Net revenue is $5 million - $2 million = $3 million. Coverage is net revenue divided by debt service: $3 million / $1.5 million = 2 to 1."},
{"id":84,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"Under a net revenue pledge, the first use of a facility's revenues is to pay:","c":["Operating and maintenance expenses","Debt service on the bonds","Deposits to the debt service reserve fund","Deposits to the renewal and replacement fund"],"e":"With a net revenue pledge, operations and maintenance are paid first and bondholders are paid from what remains. With a gross revenue pledge, debt service comes first."},
{"id":85,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A double-barreled municipal bond is:","c":["A revenue bond that is also backed by the issuer's taxing power","A bond that is exempt from both federal and state income tax","A bond issued jointly by two municipalities","A bond that carries two forms of bond insurance"],"e":"Double-barreled bonds are paid from project revenues first, with the municipality's full faith and credit as a second source of payment."},
{"id":86,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"If revenues on a moral obligation bond fall short, the state legislature:","c":["May appropriate funds to cover the shortfall, but is not legally bound to","Is legally required to raise taxes to cover the shortfall","Must call the bonds at par within one year","May not use state funds to pay the debt service"],"e":"A moral obligation bond is a revenue bond with a non-binding pledge that the legislature will consider covering shortfalls."},
{"id":87,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"The credit rating of an industrial development revenue bond is based mainly on:","c":["The corporation that leases the facility","The taxing power of the municipality","The total debt of the state","The size of the bond issue"],"e":"IDRs are issued by a municipality but paid from lease payments made by a corporation, so the corporation's credit determines the rating. Their interest may be subject to the AMT."},
{"id":88,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"What is Moody's highest rating for a short-term municipal note?","c":["MIG 1","Aaa","P-1","SP-1+"],"e":"Moody's rates municipal notes MIG 1 (best) through MIG 3, with SG for speculative grade. P-1 is its top commercial paper rating and SP-1+ is an S&P note rating."},
{"id":89,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"An investor in the 28% federal tax bracket buys a municipal bond yielding 3.6%. What is the tax-equivalent yield?","c":["5.0%","4.6%","2.6%","12.9%"],"e":"Tax-equivalent yield is the municipal yield divided by (1 - tax rate): 3.6% / 0.72 = 5.0%."},
{"id":90,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"An investor in the 35% federal tax bracket is comparing a 6% corporate bond with a 4% municipal bond. On an after-tax basis:","c":["The municipal bond yields more, because the corporate bond nets 3.9% after tax","The corporate bond yields more, because 6% is higher than 4%","The two bonds yield the same","The municipal bond nets 2.6% after tax"],"e":"The corporate bond's after-tax yield is 6% x (1 - 0.35) = 3.9%, which is below the municipal bond's tax-free 4%."},
{"id":91,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A municipal issuer publishes a notice of sale in order to:","c":["Invite competitive bids from underwriters","Announce a negotiated underwriting to investors","Give notice that outstanding bonds are being called","Tell bondholders the legal opinion is available"],"e":"The notice of sale gives bidding details for a competitive underwriting, which is the usual method for general obligation bonds."},
{"id":92,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A firm has a 10% participation in a $10 million Eastern (undivided) municipal syndicate. It sells its full $1 million allotment, but $2 million of bonds remain unsold in the syndicate. The firm's remaining liability is:","c":["$200,000","$0","$100,000","$2,000,000"],"e":"In an Eastern (undivided) account, each member is liable for its percentage of any unsold bonds no matter how much it sold: 10% x $2 million = $200,000."},
{"id":93,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A firm has a 10% participation in a $10 million Western (divided) municipal syndicate. It sells its full $1 million allotment, while other members leave $2 million of bonds unsold. The firm's remaining liability is:","c":["$0","$200,000","$100,000","$2,000,000"],"e":"In a Western (divided) account, a member that sells its own allotment has no further liability for bonds other members fail to sell."},
{"id":94,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"In a typical municipal syndicate, which order is filled first?","c":["Presale order","Group net order","Designated order","Member order"],"e":"The usual priority is presale, group net, designated, then member. Orders that benefit the whole syndicate come ahead of those that benefit individual members."},
{"id":95,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"In a municipal underwriting, the total takedown is $8 per bond and the concession is $5. A syndicate member's bond is sold to a customer by a selling group dealer. How much does the syndicate member keep?","c":["$3","$5","$8","$13"],"e":"The selling group dealer earns the $5 concession. The syndicate member keeps the rest of the takedown, called the additional takedown: $8 - $5 = $3."},
{"id":96,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"An unqualified legal opinion on a municipal bond means that bond counsel:","c":["Has no reservations about the bond's validity or tax-exempt status","Is not qualified to give an opinion on the issue","Has found problems that investors should be aware of","Guarantees that interest and principal will be paid on time"],"e":"Bond counsel's legal opinion addresses legality and tax status. An unqualified opinion is given without reservations, which is what underwriters and investors want."},
{"id":97,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"The main disclosure document for a new municipal bond issue is the:","c":["Official statement","Prospectus","Indenture","Notice of sale"],"e":"Municipal securities are exempt from SEC registration, so there is no prospectus. The official statement provides the issuer's financial and offering details."},
{"id":98,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"Interest on which bond is exempt from federal, state and local income taxes for a resident of any state?","c":["A Puerto Rico general obligation bond","A New York City general obligation bond","A U.S. Treasury bond","A GNMA certificate"],"e":"Bonds issued by U.S. territories such as Puerto Rico, Guam and the U.S. Virgin Islands are triple tax-exempt for residents of every state."},
{"id":99,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"An investor buys a new municipal bond with an original issue discount at 90 and holds it to maturity. For tax purposes, the investor has:","c":["No capital gain, because the discount accretes as tax-exempt interest","A $100 long-term capital gain per bond at maturity","$100 of taxable ordinary income per bond at maturity","A $100 short-term capital gain per bond at maturity"],"e":"The discount on an original issue discount municipal bond is accreted each year as tax-exempt interest, which raises the cost basis to par by maturity."},
{"id":100,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"An investor buys a municipal bond at 110 with 10 years to maturity. Five years later she sells it at 106. Using straight-line amortization, the result per $1,000 bond is:","c":["A $10 capital gain","A $40 capital loss","A $60 capital gain","No gain or loss"],"e":"The $100 premium is amortized at $10 a year, so the basis after five years is 105 ($1,050). Selling at 106 ($1,060) produces a $10 gain."},
{"id":101,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"Under MSRB Rule G-37, a municipal finance professional may contribute to an issuer official's campaign without triggering a ban on business if the contribution is:","c":["Up to $250 per election, to an official the professional can vote for","Up to $1,000 per election, to any official of the issuer","Made through the firm's political action committee instead","Reported to the MSRB within 30 days of the contribution"],"e":"Contributions above the $250 exception, or to officials the professional cannot vote for, bar the firm from negotiated underwriting business with that issuer for two years."},
{"id":102,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"Who enforces MSRB rules for broker-dealers?","c":["FINRA and the SEC","The MSRB itself","The Comptroller of the Currency","State banking regulators"],"e":"The MSRB writes rules but has no enforcement power. FINRA and the SEC enforce them for broker-dealers, and bank regulators enforce them for bank dealers."},
{"id":103,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"Municipal serial bonds are normally quoted:","c":["On a yield basis","As a percentage of par","In 32nds of a point","At a discount from face value"],"e":"Serial bonds are quoted by yield to maturity (a basis quote). Term bonds, called dollar bonds, are quoted as a percentage of par."},
{"id":104,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A customer buys a callable municipal bond at a premium. The yield shown on the confirmation must be:","c":["The lower of yield to call or yield to maturity","The nominal yield","The current yield","The higher of yield to call or yield to maturity"],"e":"MSRB rules require confirmations to show yield to worst, the lowest yield the customer could receive. For a premium bond callable at par, that is the yield to call."},
{"id":105,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"A municipal bond that has been pre-refunded is:","c":["Backed by U.S. government securities held in escrow and usually rated AAA","In default, with new bonds issued to replace it","No longer exempt from federal income tax","Converted from a revenue bond to a general obligation bond"],"e":"In a pre-refunding, proceeds of a new issue are placed in escrow, typically in Treasuries, to pay off the old bonds at the first call date. The old bonds' credit quality and marketability improve."},
{"id":106,"f":3,"t":"Municipals","q":"Section 529 college savings plans are:","c":["Municipal fund securities sold with an official statement","Mutual funds sold with a prospectus under FINRA rules only","Bank products covered by FDIC insurance","Exempt from all advertising rules"],"e":"529 plans are issued by states and are municipal fund securities. They fall under MSRB rules, and the disclosure document is an official statement (program disclosure)."},
{"id":107,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 1 XYZ Jul 50 call at 3. What is the breakeven price at expiration?","c":["53","47","50","56"],"e":"A long call breaks even at the strike price plus the premium: 50 + 3 = 53."},
{"id":108,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"What is the maximum possible loss for the writer of an uncovered call?","c":["Unlimited","The premium received","The strike price minus the premium","The strike price plus the premium"],"e":"The stock can rise without limit, and an uncovered writer would have to buy it at the market to deliver at the strike price."},
{"id":109,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 1 ABC Oct 40 put at 2.50. What is the maximum possible gain?","c":["$3,750","$250","$4,000","$4,250"],"e":"A long put gains the most if the stock falls to zero: (40 - 2.50) x 100 shares = $3,750. The breakeven is 37.50."},
{"id":110,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor writes 1 DEF Sep 30 put at 2 and has no other position in DEF. What is the maximum possible loss?","c":["$2,800","$200","$3,000","Unlimited"],"e":"If the stock falls to zero, the writer must still buy it at 30. The loss is the strike price less the premium received: (30 - 2) x 100 = $2,800."},
{"id":111,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"With the stock at 47, an XYZ 45 call is trading at 3.50. How much of the premium is time value?","c":["1.50","2.00","3.50","0.50"],"e":"Intrinsic value is the amount the option is in the money: 47 - 45 = 2. Time value is the rest of the premium: 3.50 - 2 = 1.50."},
{"id":112,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"MNO stock is trading at 52. Which option is in the money?","c":["MNO 55 put","MNO 55 call","MNO 50 put","MNO 60 call"],"e":"A put is in the money when the stock is below the strike price. A call is in the money when the stock is above the strike price."},
{"id":113,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 100 shares of RST at 48 and writes 1 RST 50 call at 2. What is the maximum gain?","c":["$400","$200","$4,600","Unlimited"],"e":"If the stock is called away at 50, the investor gains 2 points on the stock plus the 2-point premium: 4 x 100 = $400."},
{"id":114,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 100 shares of JKL at 62 and writes 1 JKL 65 call at 3.50. What is the breakeven?","c":["58.50","61.50","65.50","68.50"],"e":"A covered call writer breaks even at the stock's cost minus the premium received: 62 - 3.50 = 58.50."},
{"id":115,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 100 shares of UVW at 54 and buys 1 UVW 50 put at 2. What is the maximum loss?","c":["$600","$200","$400","$5,600"],"e":"The put lets the investor sell at 50, so the loss on the stock is capped at 4 points. Adding the 2-point premium gives 6 x 100 = $600."},
{"id":116,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor sells short 100 shares of GHI at 70 and buys 1 GHI 75 call at 2. What is the maximum loss?","c":["$700","$200","$500","Unlimited"],"e":"The call lets the investor buy stock at 75 to cover the short, a 5-point loss. Adding the 2-point premium gives 7 x 100 = $700."},
{"id":117,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 1 ABC 40 call at 5 and writes 1 ABC 45 call at 2, with the same expiration. What is the maximum gain?","c":["$200","$300","$500","$700"],"e":"This is a debit call spread with a net cost of 3. The maximum gain is the difference between the strikes minus the net debit: (5 - 3) x 100 = $200."},
{"id":118,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor writes 1 XYZ 60 put at 6 and buys 1 XYZ 55 put at 3, with the same expiration. What is the breakeven?","c":["57","58","63","52"],"e":"This is a credit put spread with a net credit of 3. Put spreads break even at the higher strike minus the net premium: 60 - 3 = 57."},
{"id":119,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor writes 1 LMN 30 call at 4 and buys 1 LMN 35 call at 1.50, with the same expiration. The investor profits most if:","c":["Both options expire worthless","The stock rises above 35","The difference between the two premiums widens","Both options are exercised"],"e":"This is a credit (bear) call spread. The writer keeps the full 2.50 net credit if the stock stays at or below 30 and both calls expire. Credit spreads profit as the spread narrows."},
{"id":120,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 1 QRS 80 put at 7 and writes 1 QRS 70 put at 3, with the same expiration. What is the maximum gain?","c":["$600","$400","$1,000","$300"],"e":"This is a debit (bear) put spread with a net cost of 4. The maximum gain is the 10-point difference in strikes minus the debit: (10 - 4) x 100 = $600."},
{"id":121,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor is long 1 XYZ Jan 50 call and short 1 XYZ Apr 50 call. This position is a:","c":["Calendar (horizontal) spread","Vertical (price) spread","Diagonal spread","Straddle"],"e":"The options have the same strike price and different expirations, which makes it a horizontal spread. A diagonal spread differs in both strike and expiration."},
{"id":122,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 1 DEF 50 call at 3 and 1 DEF 50 put at 2.50, with the same expiration. What are the breakeven points?","c":["44.50 and 55.50","47 and 52.50","47.50 and 53","45 and 55"],"e":"A long straddle breaks even at the strike price plus and minus the total premium: 50 + 5.50 and 50 - 5.50."},
{"id":123,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor who writes a straddle expects the stock to:","c":["Trade in a narrow range","Rise sharply","Fall sharply","Move sharply, in either direction"],"e":"A short straddle earns the most when the stock stays at the strike price and both options expire worthless. A sharp move in either direction produces losses."},
{"id":124,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor owns stock, buys an out-of-the-money put on it and writes an out-of-the-money call on it. The purpose of this collar is to:","c":["Limit downside risk cheaply by giving up gains above the call's strike","Profit from a sharp price move in either direction","Increase leverage on the underlying stock position","Earn premium income with no cap on potential gains"],"e":"The call premium helps pay for the protective put. The investor is protected below the put's strike and gives up gains above the call's strike."},
{"id":125,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"When a call holder exercises, the Options Clearing Corporation assigns the exercise notice:","c":["To a broker-dealer chosen at random","To the broker-dealer with the oldest short position","To the writer who received the largest premium","To the exchange where the option trades"],"e":"The OCC assigns exercise notices to firms randomly. The firm then assigns a customer using random selection, first-in first-out, or another fair method."},
{"id":126,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"Standard monthly equity options expire at:","c":["11:59 p.m. Eastern time on the third Friday of the expiration month","4:00 p.m. Eastern time on the third Friday of the expiration month","5:30 p.m. Eastern time on the third Saturday of the expiration month","The close of trading on the last business day of the month"],"e":"Trading in the option stops at 4:00 p.m. ET on the third Friday. The contract itself expires at 11:59 p.m. ET that day."},
{"id":127,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"A customer exercises an equity call option on Tuesday. When does the resulting stock purchase settle?","c":["Wednesday","Tuesday","Thursday","The following Tuesday"],"e":"Stock delivered through the exercise of an equity option settles regular way, one business day after exercise (T+1)."},
{"id":128,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor exercises 1 American-style index call with a strike price of 480. The index closes at 492 that day. The investor receives:","c":["$1,200 in cash","100 shares of each stock in the index","$49,200 in cash","$1,200 worth of index shares"],"e":"Index options settle in cash. The holder receives the in-the-money amount, based on the closing index value on the day of exercise, times 100: (492 - 480) x 100."},
{"id":129,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"A portfolio manager wants to hedge a $1,000,000 stock portfolio with a beta of 1.2 using index puts. The index is at 500 and the multiplier is 100. How many puts are needed?","c":["24","20","17","12"],"e":"Each contract covers 500 x $100 = $50,000. $1,000,000 / $50,000 = 20 contracts, and adjusting for the 1.2 beta gives 24."},
{"id":130,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor is long 1 XYZ 60 call. XYZ splits 2 for 1. After the adjustment the investor owns:","c":["2 XYZ 30 calls, each for 100 shares","1 XYZ 30 call for 200 shares","2 XYZ 60 calls, each for 100 shares","1 XYZ 60 call for 100 shares"],"e":"For an even split, the number of contracts increases and the strike price is reduced proportionally. Each contract still covers 100 shares."},
{"id":131,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor is long 1 XYZ 60 call. XYZ splits 3 for 2. After the adjustment the investor owns:","c":["1 XYZ 40 call for 150 shares","1.5 XYZ 40 calls, each for 100 shares","1 XYZ 60 call for 150 shares","3 XYZ 20 calls, each for 100 shares"],"e":"For an uneven split, the number of contracts stays the same. The shares per contract increase (100 x 3/2 = 150) and the strike is reduced (60 x 2/3 = 40)."},
{"id":132,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"A company pays its regular quarterly cash dividend. Listed options on its stock are:","c":["Not adjusted","Adjusted by lowering the strike price by the dividend amount","Adjusted by increasing the number of shares per contract","Cancelled and reissued"],"e":"Listed options are adjusted for stock splits and stock dividends. They are not adjusted for ordinary cash dividends."},
{"id":133,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"A U.S. importer must pay a supplier in euros in three months and is worried the euro will strengthen. Which listed currency option position is the best hedge?","c":["Buy euro calls","Buy euro puts","Write euro calls","Buy U.S. dollar calls"],"e":"If the euro rises, the importer's cost in dollars goes up. Long euro calls gain in value as the euro rises. Listed currency options are on the foreign currency, not on the dollar."},
{"id":134,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor expects interest rates to rise. Which yield-based option position would profit?","c":["Buy yield-based calls","Buy yield-based puts","Write yield-based calls","Buy calls on Treasury bond prices"],"e":"Yield-based options move with interest rates rather than bond prices. If rates rise, yield-based calls gain value."},
{"id":135,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor buys 1 XYZ 50 call at 4 and later exercises it. What is the cost basis of the stock per share?","c":["$54","$50","$46","$4"],"e":"When a call is exercised, the premium paid is added to the strike price to find the cost basis of the stock: 50 + 4 = 54."},
{"id":136,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor writes 1 ABC 40 put at 3 and is later assigned. What is the cost basis of the stock per share?","c":["$37","$40","$43","$3"],"e":"A put writer who is assigned must buy the stock at the strike price. The premium received reduces the cost basis: 40 - 3 = 37."},
{"id":137,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"An investor wrote a call last month and now wants to eliminate the position. The order should be marked:","c":["Closing purchase","Closing sale","Opening purchase","Opening sale"],"e":"The writer established the position with an opening sale. Buying the same option back to end the obligation is a closing purchase."},
{"id":138,"f":3,"t":"Options","q":"Which pair of positions would both profit from a decline in the underlying stock?","c":["Long put and short call","Long call and short put","Long call and long put","Short call and short put"],"e":"Put buyers and call writers are bearish. Call buyers and put writers are bullish."},
{"id":139,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A mutual fund has total assets of $52 million, liabilities of $2 million and 5 million shares outstanding. What is its net asset value per share?","c":["$10.00","$10.40","$10.80","$9.60"],"e":"NAV per share is (assets - liabilities) divided by shares outstanding: ($52 million - $2 million) / 5 million = $10.00."},
{"id":140,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A mutual fund has an NAV of $18.40 and a sales charge of 8%. What is the public offering price?","c":["$20.00","$19.87","$16.93","$18.55"],"e":"The sales charge is a percentage of the offering price, so POP = NAV / (1 - sales charge) = $18.40 / 0.92 = $20.00."},
{"id":141,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A mutual fund has an NAV of $9.50 and a public offering price of $10.00. What is the sales charge percentage?","c":["5.0%","5.26%","0.5%","9.5%"],"e":"Sales charge percentage is (POP - NAV) divided by POP: $0.50 / $10.00 = 5%."},
{"id":142,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A customer enters an order to buy mutual fund shares at 2:00 p.m. Eastern time. The price she pays is based on:","c":["The NAV calculated at that day's market close","The NAV calculated at the previous day's close","The NAV at the moment the order was entered","The NAV at the next day's market open"],"e":"Mutual funds use forward pricing. Orders are filled at the next NAV calculated after the order is received, normally at the 4:00 p.m. close."},
{"id":143,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A letter of intent for a mutual fund purchase:","c":["Covers 13 months and may be backdated 90 days","Covers 12 months and may not be backdated","Legally obligates the investor to complete the purchases","Covers 24 months and may be backdated 30 days"],"e":"An LOI lets an investor qualify for a breakpoint by pledging to invest a set amount within 13 months. It can be backdated up to 90 days and is not binding on the investor."},
{"id":144,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"Rights of accumulation allow a mutual fund investor to:","c":["Count shares already held toward a breakpoint on new purchases","Buy additional shares at net asset value with no sales charge","Exchange shares within a fund family without owing tax","Redeem shares without paying a contingent deferred sales charge"],"e":"Rights of accumulation count existing holdings toward a breakpoint for new purchases. Unlike a letter of intent, there is no time limit."},
{"id":145,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A representative recommends that a customer invest $24,000 in a fund that offers a reduced sales charge at $25,000, without mentioning the breakpoint. This is:","c":["A breakpoint sale, which is prohibited","Acceptable, since the customer chose the amount","Acceptable if the fund is no-load","Required to be reported as a private securities transaction"],"e":"Encouraging purchases just below a breakpoint so the customer pays a higher sales charge is a prohibited practice known as a breakpoint sale."},
{"id":146,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A customer plans to invest $500,000 in an equity mutual fund and hold it for at least 15 years. Which share class is generally most suitable?","c":["Class A shares","Class B shares","Class C shares","An equal mix of Class B and Class C shares"],"e":"A large purchase qualifies for breakpoints that reduce or eliminate the Class A front-end load, and Class A shares have the lowest ongoing 12b-1 fees for a long holding period."},
{"id":147,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"Class C mutual fund shares are typically most appropriate for an investor who:","c":["Has a short investment time horizon","Is investing a large sum for 20 years","Wants the lowest ongoing expenses","Qualifies for the highest breakpoint"],"e":"Class C shares have little or no front-end load but charge higher level 12b-1 fees every year, which becomes expensive over long holding periods."},
{"id":148,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A mutual fund may describe itself as no-load only if it has no sales charge and its 12b-1 fee does not exceed:","c":["0.25% of average net assets","0.75% of average net assets","1.00% of average net assets","8.5% of the offering price"],"e":"A fund with no front-end or back-end sales charge can be called no-load as long as its 12b-1 and service fees total 0.25% or less per year."},
{"id":149,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"Which statement about closed-end funds is accurate?","c":["Their shares trade in the secondary market and can be priced above or below NAV","They continuously issue new shares at NAV plus a sales charge","Their shares are redeemed by the fund at NAV","They may not issue preferred stock or bonds"],"e":"A closed-end fund issues a fixed number of shares that then trade on an exchange. Supply and demand set the price, so it can be at a premium or discount to NAV."},
{"id":150,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A unit investment trust differs from a mutual fund because the UIT:","c":["Has a fixed portfolio with no investment adviser","Does not redeem its units","Trades only on an exchange","Is not regulated under the Investment Company Act of 1940"],"e":"A UIT holds a fixed portfolio that is not actively managed, so there is no adviser and no management fee. Its units are redeemable."},
{"id":151,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"Compared with a traditional mutual fund, shares of an exchange-traded fund:","c":["Trade throughout the day and can be bought on margin or sold short","Are priced once a day at the close","Are purchased directly from the fund at NAV plus a sales charge","May not be held in retirement accounts"],"e":"ETF shares trade on exchanges at market prices during the day, like stocks. Investors pay commissions rather than sales charges and can use margin or sell short."},
{"id":152,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"A leveraged ETF that aims for twice the daily return of an index is generally:","c":["Suited to short-term trading, since it resets its leverage every day","Suited to buy-and-hold investors saving for retirement","Guaranteed to return twice the index over any holding period","Less volatile over time than the index that it tracks"],"e":"Leveraged and inverse ETFs reset every day. Over longer periods, compounding can make returns differ widely from the stated multiple, so they are generally unsuitable for long-term holding."},
{"id":153,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"Under Subchapter M of the Internal Revenue Code, a mutual fund avoids tax on the income it distributes if it distributes at least:","c":["90% of its net investment income","50% of its net investment income","75% of its net investment income","100% of its capital gains"],"e":"A regulated investment company that passes through at least 90% of net investment income is taxed only on what it retains. Shareholders pay tax on the distributions."},
{"id":154,"f":3,"t":"Funds","q":"After a shareholder asks to redeem mutual fund shares, the fund must send payment within:","c":["Seven calendar days","One business day","Three business days","Thirty calendar days"],"e":"The Investment Company Act of 1940 requires redemption proceeds to be paid within seven calendar days of the request."},
{"id":155,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"In a variable annuity, the investment risk is carried by:","c":["The contract owner","The insurance company","The broker-dealer that sold the contract","The state insurance guaranty fund"],"e":"Variable annuity payments depend on the performance of the separate account, so the owner bears the investment risk. In a fixed annuity the insurer bears it."},
{"id":156,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"When a variable annuity is annuitized, the owner's accumulation units are converted into:","c":["A fixed number of annuity units whose value changes over time","A fixed dollar payment for life","Shares of the insurer's common stock","A changing number of annuity units, each with a fixed value"],"e":"At annuitization the number of annuity units is fixed. The value of each unit rises or falls with the separate account, so the payment amount varies."},
{"id":157,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"A variable annuity has an assumed interest rate (AIR) of 4%. Last month the separate account earned 7% annualized, and this month it earns 4%. This month's payment will be:","c":["The same as last month's payment","Higher than last month's payment","Lower than last month's payment","Equal to the very first payment"],"e":"Payments are compared with the previous month. When the separate account return equals the AIR, the payment stays the same as the prior month."},
{"id":158,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"Which annuity payout option provides the largest periodic payment?","c":["Life only (straight life)","Life with 10-year period certain","Joint and last survivor","Unit refund life"],"e":"A straight life annuity stops at the annuitant's death with nothing for beneficiaries. Because the insurer's risk is lowest, the payment is highest."},
{"id":159,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"A 50-year-old invested $40,000 in a non-qualified variable annuity that is now worth $65,000. She takes a $15,000 withdrawal. The tax result is:","c":["$15,000 of ordinary income plus a 10% penalty","No tax, because it is a return of her contributions","$15,000 of long-term capital gain","$5,769 of ordinary income, taxed pro rata"],"e":"Withdrawals from non-qualified annuities are taxed last-in, first-out, so earnings come out first as ordinary income. Before age 59 1/2 a 10% penalty also applies to the taxable amount."},
{"id":160,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"Which exchange does NOT qualify as a tax-free exchange under Section 1035?","c":["An annuity contract into a life insurance policy","A life insurance policy into an annuity contract","An annuity contract into another annuity contract","A life insurance policy into another life insurance policy"],"e":"Section 1035 permits life-to-life, life-to-annuity and annuity-to-annuity exchanges. An annuity cannot be exchanged tax-free into life insurance."},
{"id":161,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"A customer wants to buy a variable annuity inside her traditional IRA. The representative should point out that:","c":["The IRA is already tax-deferred, so the annuity adds no tax benefit","Annuities may not be held in an IRA","The purchase will make her IRA contributions tax-free","The annuity removes the IRA's required minimum distributions"],"e":"Tax deferral is a key selling point of annuities, and an IRA already provides it. The recommendation needs to be justified by other features, such as the death benefit or lifetime income."},
{"id":162,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"In a variable life insurance policy:","c":["A minimum death benefit is guaranteed, but the cash value is not","Both the death benefit and the cash value are guaranteed","Neither the death benefit nor the cash value has any guarantee","The cash value is guaranteed, but the death benefit is not"],"e":"Variable life has fixed premiums and a guaranteed minimum death benefit. The cash value depends on separate account performance and can fall to zero."},
{"id":163,"f":3,"t":"Annuities & insurance","q":"The owner of a variable annuity dies during the accumulation phase. The beneficiary generally receives:","c":["The greater of the total contributions or the current account value","Only the amount contributed, without earnings","The account value minus a 10% early withdrawal penalty","Nothing, because the contract was not annuitized"],"e":"The standard death benefit pays the larger of the amount invested or the contract's current value. Any gain is ordinary income to the beneficiary."},
{"id":164,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"A distribution of earnings from a Roth IRA is tax-free if the owner is at least 59 1/2 and the account has been open for at least:","c":["Five years","One year","Three years","Ten years"],"e":"A qualified Roth distribution requires both the five-year holding period and a qualifying event such as reaching age 59 1/2, death or disability."},
{"id":165,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"A 45-year-old takes money from her traditional IRA. Which use of the funds avoids the 10% early withdrawal penalty?","c":["Paying qualified higher education expenses","Buying a car for commuting","Paying off credit card debt","Making a down payment on a vacation home"],"e":"Exceptions to the IRA early withdrawal penalty include qualified higher education costs, a first-time home purchase (up to $10,000), death and disability. Ordinary income tax still applies."},
{"id":166,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"An IRA owner receives a distribution check and wants to roll it into another IRA. Which statement is accurate?","c":["The rollover must be completed within 60 days and is allowed once in a 12-month period","The rollover must be completed within 30 days and is allowed once per calendar quarter","There is no limit on the number of rollovers per year","The rollover must be completed within 90 days"],"e":"A 60-day rollover is limited to one per 12 months. Direct trustee-to-trustee transfers are unlimited."},
{"id":167,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"An employee leaves her job and has her 401(k) balance paid to her directly by check. The plan must withhold:","c":["20% for federal income tax","10% for federal income tax","Nothing, if she completes a rollover within 60 days","25% for federal income tax"],"e":"Eligible rollover distributions paid to the participant are subject to mandatory 20% withholding. A direct rollover to an IRA or another plan avoids it."},
{"id":168,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"Which of these may NOT be held in an IRA?","c":["Antiques","Covered call positions","U.S.-minted gold coins","Real estate investment trusts"],"e":"IRAs cannot invest in collectibles such as art, antiques and most coins, or in life insurance. U.S.-minted gold and silver coins are an exception."},
{"id":169,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"ERISA rules apply to:","c":["Private-sector employer retirement plans","Retirement plans for federal government employees","Retirement plans for state and local government employees","Individual retirement accounts opened by individuals"],"e":"ERISA protects participants in private-sector (corporate) retirement plans with rules on funding, vesting, participation and fiduciary conduct."},
{"id":170,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"In a defined benefit pension plan:","c":["The employer bears the investment risk","Each employee bears the investment risk","Benefits depend only on the plan's investment results","Contributions are a fixed percentage of each employee's pay"],"e":"A defined benefit plan promises a set retirement benefit. The employer must fund it, using actuarial calculations, whatever the investment results are."},
{"id":171,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"A 403(b) plan is available to employees of:","c":["Public schools and certain tax-exempt organizations","Any corporation with fewer than 100 employees","State and local governments only","Self-employed individuals only"],"e":"403(b) plans, also called tax-sheltered annuities, cover employees of public schools and 501(c)(3) organizations."},
{"id":172,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"Which statement about Section 529 college savings plans is accurate?","c":["Qualified education withdrawals are free of federal income tax","Contributions are deductible on the federal tax return","The beneficiary takes control of the account at age 18","Unused funds must be withdrawn by the time the beneficiary is 30"],"e":"529 contributions are made with after-tax dollars at the federal level, and qualified withdrawals are federally tax-free. The account owner keeps control and can change the beneficiary."},
{"id":173,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"Contributions to a Coverdell Education Savings Account are limited to:","c":["$2,000 per year for each beneficiary","$2,000 per year for each contributor","$5,000 per year for each beneficiary","The annual gift tax exclusion amount"],"e":"The Coverdell limit is $2,000 per beneficiary per year in total. Contributions end at age 18 and the funds must generally be used by age 30."},
{"id":174,"f":3,"t":"Retirement & education","q":"Which account has no required minimum distributions during the original owner's lifetime?","c":["Roth IRA","Traditional IRA","SEP IRA","SIMPLE IRA"],"e":"Roth IRA owners never have to take distributions during their lifetime. Traditional, SEP and SIMPLE IRAs all have required minimum distributions."},
{"id":175,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"A key tax feature of a limited partnership is that:","c":["Income and losses pass through to the partners","The partnership pays tax at the corporate rate","Losses can be used to offset the partners' salaries","Distributions are taxed as qualified dividends"],"e":"A direct participation program is not a taxable entity. Income, gains, losses, deductions and credits flow through to the partners' own tax returns."},
{"id":176,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"Losses passed through from a limited partnership may be used to offset:","c":["Passive income only","Salary and wages","Interest and dividends","Capital gains on stock"],"e":"Limited partnership losses are passive losses. They can be deducted only against passive income, such as income from other partnerships or rental real estate."},
{"id":177,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"Which activity could cause a limited partner to lose limited liability?","c":["Making day-to-day management decisions for the partnership","Voting to remove the general partner","Inspecting the partnership's books","Suing the general partner"],"e":"Limited partners are passive investors. Taking part in management can cause them to be treated as general partners with unlimited liability."},
{"id":178,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"When a limited partnership is liquidated, who is paid last?","c":["General partners","Limited partners","Secured lenders","General creditors"],"e":"The order is secured lenders, general creditors, limited partners, then general partners."},
{"id":179,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"Which oil and gas program carries the highest risk?","c":["Exploratory (wildcat) drilling","Developmental drilling","An income program that buys producing wells","A combination program"],"e":"Exploratory programs drill in unproven areas, so they have the highest risk and the highest potential reward. Income programs buy existing production and have the lowest risk."},
{"id":180,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"A limited partnership that invests in raw land offers investors:","c":["Possible appreciation, with no income or depreciation","High current income from rental payments","Large depreciation deductions each year","Tax credits for low-income housing"],"e":"Raw land is a speculative investment held for appreciation. It produces no rental income and land cannot be depreciated."},
{"id":181,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"An investor in a limited partnership offering becomes a limited partner when:","c":["The general partner accepts and signs the subscription agreement","The investor signs the subscription agreement","The investor's check clears","The certificate of limited partnership is filed with the state"],"e":"The subscription agreement is the investor's application. It takes effect only when the general partner accepts it."},
{"id":182,"f":3,"t":"DPPs","q":"In a limited partnership, the crossover point is reached when:","c":["The program begins to generate taxable income instead of tax losses","The limited partners have recovered their original investment","The general partner's capital equals the limited partners' capital","The partnership's debt has been fully repaid"],"e":"In the early years deductions usually exceed revenue and the program passes through losses. The crossover point is when taxable income begins to exceed deductions."},
{"id":183,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A customer with an existing margin account buys $20,000 of marginable stock. Under Regulation T, she must deposit:","c":["$10,000","$5,000","$20,000","$2,000"],"e":"Regulation T requires an initial deposit of 50% of the purchase price for marginable equity securities."},
{"id":184,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A customer's first trade in a new margin account is a $3,000 stock purchase. How much must be deposited?","c":["$2,000","$1,500","$3,000","$750"],"e":"FINRA requires minimum equity of $2,000 for a first purchase on margin. The customer deposits the greater of the Reg T requirement ($1,500) or $2,000, but never more than the full price."},
{"id":185,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A customer's first trade in a new margin account is a $1,600 stock purchase. How much must be deposited?","c":["$1,600","$800","$2,000","$400"],"e":"When the first purchase is under $2,000, the customer pays in full. The $2,000 minimum never requires depositing more than the purchase price."},
{"id":186,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A customer's first trade in a new margin account is a short sale of $1,500 of stock. How much must be deposited?","c":["$2,000","$750","$1,500","$3,000"],"e":"Short sales always require at least $2,000 of equity, even when the stock sold short is worth less than that."},
{"id":187,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A margin account has a long market value of $50,000 and a debit balance of $20,000. What is the excess equity (SMA)?","c":["$5,000","$30,000","$10,000","$17,500"],"e":"Equity is $50,000 - $20,000 = $30,000. The Reg T requirement is 50% of $50,000 = $25,000, so excess equity is $5,000."},
{"id":188,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A margin account has $5,000 of SMA. How much marginable stock can the customer buy without depositing more money?","c":["$10,000","$5,000","$2,500","$20,000"],"e":"With Reg T at 50%, buying power is twice the SMA: $5,000 x 2 = $10,000."},
{"id":189,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A long margin account has a debit balance of $30,000. To what market value can the securities fall before a maintenance call is issued?","c":["$40,000","$37,500","$60,000","$45,000"],"e":"At the 25% minimum maintenance level, the debit balance equals 75% of market value. $30,000 / 0.75 = $40,000."},
{"id":190,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A long margin account has a market value of $40,000 and a debit balance of $32,000. What is the maintenance call?","c":["$2,000","$8,000","$12,000","No call is due"],"e":"Equity is $8,000, or 20%. Minimum maintenance is 25% of $40,000 = $10,000, so the customer must deposit $2,000."},
{"id":191,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A customer sells short $40,000 of stock and deposits the $20,000 Reg T requirement. The stock's market value then rises to $45,000. What is the equity in the account?","c":["$15,000","$20,000","$25,000","$5,000"],"e":"The credit balance is $40,000 + $20,000 = $60,000. Short account equity is the credit balance minus the short market value: $60,000 - $45,000 = $15,000."},
{"id":192,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A short margin account has a credit balance of $65,000. To what market value can the short position rise before a maintenance call is issued?","c":["$50,000","$45,500","$86,667","$48,750"],"e":"Minimum maintenance on a short position is 30%. The maximum short market value is the credit balance divided by 1.3: $65,000 / 1.3 = $50,000."},
{"id":193,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"The market value of the securities in a long margin account falls. What happens to the SMA?","c":["It does not change","It falls dollar for dollar with the market value","It falls by 50% of the decline","It is eliminated"],"e":"SMA is a line of credit that rises when market value rises. A market decline does not reduce it, although it cannot be used if doing so would put the account below maintenance."},
{"id":194,"f":3,"t":"Margin","q":"A customer's margin account has a debit balance of $10,000. What is the maximum value of the customer's securities that the broker-dealer may pledge for a bank loan?","c":["$14,000","$10,000","$5,000","$20,000"],"e":"A broker-dealer may rehypothecate customer securities worth up to 140% of the customer's debit balance. The rest must be segregated."},
{"id":195,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"A 68-year-old retiree wants to preserve principal and receive dependable income. Which recommendation is most suitable?","c":["A ladder of Treasury notes and investment-grade bonds","A portfolio of small-cap growth stocks","An exploratory oil and gas limited partnership","Long positions in index call options"],"e":"High-quality bonds match the goals of capital preservation and income. The other choices are aimed at growth or speculation and put principal at greater risk."},
{"id":196,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"Which investment exposes an investor to the most purchasing power risk?","c":["A 30-year fixed-rate corporate bond","Common stock","Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities","A variable annuity invested in equities"],"e":"Purchasing power (inflation) risk is greatest for long-term fixed payments, because the payments stay the same while prices rise."},
{"id":197,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"An investor who relies on bond coupon payments faces the most reinvestment risk when:","c":["Interest rates are falling","Interest rates are rising","The yield curve is steep","Stock prices are falling"],"e":"Reinvestment risk is the risk that interest and principal payments will have to be reinvested at lower rates than the original investment earned."},
{"id":198,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"Diversifying a stock portfolio across many industries reduces:","c":["Nonsystematic (business) risk","Systematic (market) risk","Interest rate risk","Purchasing power risk"],"e":"Diversification reduces risk that is specific to one company or industry. Systematic risk affects the whole market and cannot be diversified away."},
{"id":199,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"A stock has a beta of 1.5. If the overall market rises 10%, the stock would be expected to:","c":["Rise about 15%","Rise about 10%","Rise about 5%","Fall about 15%"],"e":"Beta measures a stock's volatility relative to the market. A beta of 1.5 means it is expected to move 1.5 times as much as the market, in the same direction."},
{"id":200,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"A customer says she may need to get her money back out on short notice. Which investment is least suitable?","c":["A limited partnership interest","A money market fund","Treasury bills","Shares of a large-cap stock listed on the NYSE"],"e":"Limited partnership interests have little or no secondary market, so they carry significant liquidity risk."},
{"id":201,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"Which investment is generally least appropriate inside a traditional IRA?","c":["A municipal bond fund","A growth stock fund","A corporate bond fund","A Treasury note"],"e":"Municipal bonds pay lower yields because their interest is tax-exempt. In an IRA that benefit is wasted, and withdrawals are taxed as ordinary income anyway."},
{"id":202,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"An investor builds a bond portfolio with equal amounts maturing every year for the next ten years. This strategy is called a:","c":["Ladder","Barbell","Bullet","Swap"],"e":"A laddered portfolio spreads maturities evenly. As each bond matures the proceeds are reinvested at current rates, which reduces interest rate and reinvestment risk."},
{"id":203,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"A portfolio manager temporarily shifts a client's mix from 60% stocks to 70% stocks because she expects a market rally. This is an example of:","c":["Tactical asset allocation","Strategic asset allocation","Passive indexing","Dollar-cost averaging"],"e":"Tactical allocation makes short-term changes to take advantage of expected market conditions. Strategic allocation sets the long-term target mix."},
{"id":204,"f":3,"t":"Suitability","q":"An investor puts $600 into a mutual fund each month. Over three months the share prices are $20, $30 and $15. What is her average cost per share?","c":["$20.00","$21.67","$22.50","$18.00"],"e":"She buys 30, 20 and 40 shares, for 90 shares in total. Average cost is $1,800 / 90 = $20.00, which is below the average price of $21.67. That is the benefit of dollar-cost averaging."},
{"id":205,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"A company has current assets of $4 million and current liabilities of $2.5 million. What is its working capital?","c":["$1.5 million","$6.5 million","$1.6 million","$2.5 million"],"e":"Working capital is current assets minus current liabilities: $4 million - $2.5 million = $1.5 million."},
{"id":206,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"The quick (acid-test) ratio differs from the current ratio because it excludes:","c":["Inventory","Cash","Accounts receivable","Accounts payable"],"e":"The quick ratio is (current assets - inventory) divided by current liabilities. It is a stricter test of liquidity because inventory may be hard to convert to cash quickly."},
{"id":207,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"A stock trades at $60 and the company earned $4 per share over the last year. What is the price-to-earnings ratio?","c":["15","6.7","24","240"],"e":"P/E is market price divided by earnings per share: $60 / $4 = 15."},
{"id":208,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"A company earns $4.00 per share and pays an annual dividend of $1.20 per share. What is its dividend payout ratio?","c":["30%","3.3%","70%","12%"],"e":"The dividend payout ratio is dividends per share divided by earnings per share: $1.20 / $4.00 = 30%."},
{"id":209,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"When a corporation declares a cash dividend, its working capital:","c":["Decreases","Increases","Does not change","Decreases only when the dividend is paid"],"e":"Declaring a dividend creates a current liability (dividends payable), which reduces working capital. Paying it later reduces cash and the liability equally, so working capital does not change again."},
{"id":210,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"Which of these is a leading economic indicator?","c":["Building permits","Duration of unemployment","Industrial production","Corporate profits"],"e":"Leading indicators such as building permits, stock prices and new orders tend to change before the economy does. Industrial production is coincident, and unemployment duration and corporate profits are lagging."},
{"id":211,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"To ease credit and stimulate the economy, the Federal Reserve would most likely:","c":["Buy Treasury securities in the open market","Sell Treasury securities in the open market","Raise the discount rate","Raise reserve requirements"],"e":"When the Fed buys securities, it adds reserves to the banking system. This increases the money supply and pushes interest rates down."},
{"id":212,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"The federal funds rate is the rate charged on:","c":["Overnight loans of reserves between banks","Loans from the Federal Reserve to member banks","Bank loans to their most creditworthy corporate customers","Bank loans to broker-dealers secured by customer securities"],"e":"The fed funds rate applies to overnight lending between banks. The discount rate is charged by the Fed, the prime rate applies to top corporate borrowers, and the broker call rate applies to margin lending."},
{"id":213,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"Which industry is considered defensive?","c":["Utilities","Automobiles","Steel","Home building"],"e":"Defensive industries such as utilities, food and pharmaceuticals are less affected by the business cycle. Autos, steel and housing are cyclical."},
{"id":214,"f":3,"t":"Analysis & economics","q":"A stock has fallen to about $30 several times and bounced back each time. A technical analyst would call $30 the stock's:","c":["Support level","Resistance level","Breakout point","Moving average"],"e":"Support is a price where buying has repeatedly stopped a decline. Resistance is a price where selling has repeatedly stopped an advance."},
{"id":215,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"An investor buys stock on March 3. What is the first date she can sell it and have the gain treated as long-term?","c":["March 4 of the following year","March 3 of the following year","September 4 of the same year","March 2 of the following year"],"e":"A long-term gain requires a holding period of more than one year. The holding period starts the day after the purchase, so the sale must be at least one year and one day later."},
{"id":216,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"An investor sells 100 shares of XYZ at a $500 loss. Twenty days later she buys 100 shares of XYZ at $30. What is the cost basis of the new shares?","c":["$35 per share","$30 per share","$25 per share","$530 per share"],"e":"Buying back within 30 days makes this a wash sale. The $500 loss is disallowed and added to the basis of the new shares: $30 + $5 = $35."},
{"id":217,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"An investor inherits stock from her father. Her cost basis is:","c":["The market value on the date of his death, and any gain is long-term","Her father's original cost, and his holding period carries over","The market value on the date of his death, and her holding period starts that day","Zero, so the entire sale price is taxable"],"e":"Inherited securities receive a stepped-up basis equal to fair market value at death, and the heir's holding period is always treated as long-term."},
{"id":218,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"A father gives his daughter stock that has gone up in value since he bought it. If she later sells at a gain, her cost basis is:","c":["Her father's original cost","The market value on the date of the gift","The market value on the date she sells","Zero"],"e":"For a gift of appreciated securities, the recipient takes the donor's cost basis and holding period."},
{"id":219,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"An investor has $4,000 of capital gains and $12,000 of capital losses for the year. How much of the net loss can she deduct against ordinary income this year?","c":["$3,000, with $5,000 carried forward","$8,000","$3,000, with the rest lost","$12,000"],"e":"The net capital loss is $8,000. Up to $3,000 per year can be deducted against ordinary income, and the remaining $5,000 carries forward indefinitely."},
{"id":220,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"An investor buys a municipal bond at par and sells it two years later at 104. The $40 profit per bond is:","c":["A taxable long-term capital gain","Tax-exempt interest","A tax-exempt capital gain","Ordinary income"],"e":"Only the interest on municipal bonds is tax-exempt. Capital gains on municipal bonds are taxable."},
{"id":221,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"A corporation owns less than 20% of another domestic corporation and receives dividends from it. What portion of those dividends is excluded from the recipient's taxable income?","c":["50%","30%","70%","100%"],"e":"The corporate dividends-received deduction is 50% when the recipient owns less than 20% of the paying corporation."},
{"id":222,"f":3,"t":"Taxation","q":"A mutual fund shareholder automatically reinvests all dividends and capital gains distributions in a taxable account. The distributions are:","c":["Taxable in the year they are paid, and they increase her cost basis","Not taxable until she sells the shares","Taxable only if she holds the fund less than one year","Tax-free, because no cash was received"],"e":"Reinvested distributions are taxed the same as distributions taken in cash. Because tax has been paid on them, they are added to the cost basis."},
{"id":223,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A buy stop order is placed:","c":["Above the current market price, often to protect a short position","Below the current market price, to buy on a dip","Above the current market price, to guarantee a purchase price","Below the current market price, to protect a long position"],"e":"A buy stop is entered above the market and becomes a market order when the stock trades at or above the stop price. It limits losses on a short sale or buys on a breakout."},
{"id":224,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A customer owns stock trading at $50 and enters a sell stop order at $45. If the stock trades at $45, the order:","c":["Becomes a market order and may be filled above or below $45","Is guaranteed to be filled at $45","Becomes a limit order to sell at $45 or higher","Is cancelled"],"e":"Once triggered, a stop order becomes a market order. It will be executed, but the price is not guaranteed."},
{"id":225,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A customer enters a sell stop-limit order at 40 stop, 39.50 limit. Trades then occur at 40.10, 39.90, 39.40, 39.45 and 39.60. At what price is the order filled?","c":["39.60","39.90","39.40","40.10"],"e":"The order is triggered at 39.90, the first trade at or below 40. It then becomes a limit order to sell at 39.50 or better, and the first such trade is 39.60."},
{"id":226,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"On the ex-dividend date for a cash dividend, which open order is reduced by the amount of the dividend?","c":["A buy limit order","A sell limit order","A buy stop order","A market order"],"e":"Open orders entered below the market, which are buy limits and sell stops, are reduced on the ex-date unless they are marked do not reduce (DNR)."},
{"id":227,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A customer wants to buy 5,000 shares at $20. She wants any shares available right now and wants the unfilled part cancelled. Which order should be entered?","c":["Immediate-or-cancel","Fill-or-kill","All-or-none","Good-til-cancelled"],"e":"An IOC order is executed immediately for as many shares as possible, and the rest is cancelled. Fill-or-kill requires the entire order to be filled immediately."},
{"id":228,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"Which statement comparing market orders and limit orders is accurate?","c":["A market order guarantees execution, and a limit order guarantees a price or better","A market order guarantees a price, and a limit order guarantees execution","Both guarantee execution","Both guarantee a price"],"e":"A market order will be filled, but at whatever price is available. A limit order will only be filled at the limit price or better, so it may never be filled."},
{"id":229,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"Before accepting a short sale order in an equity security, Regulation SHO requires the firm to:","c":["Have reasonable grounds to believe the shares can be borrowed","Confirm that the last trade was an uptick","Collect the full sale proceeds from the customer in advance","Obtain approval from the issuer of the stock"],"e":"This is the locate requirement. The firm must document that the shares can be borrowed for delivery before it executes the short sale."},
{"id":230,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A stock falls 10% from the previous day's close. Under Regulation SHO Rule 201, short sales in that stock:","c":["Must be priced above the national best bid for the rest of that day and the next","Are banned entirely for the rest of that day and the next","May be executed only on a plus tick for the next five trading days","Are not restricted unless the stock falls 20%"],"e":"The short sale circuit breaker is triggered by a 10% decline. Short sales must then be priced above the best bid for the rest of the day and the following trading day."},
{"id":231,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"Under Regulation SHO, every equity sell order must be marked:","c":["Long, short or short exempt","Solicited or unsolicited only","Agency or principal only","Day or good-til-cancelled only"],"e":"Sell order tickets must show whether the sale is long, short or short exempt."},
{"id":232,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"The third market is where:","c":["Exchange-listed securities trade over the counter","Institutions trade directly with each other without a broker-dealer","New issues are sold to the public","Unlisted securities trade over the counter"],"e":"The third market is OTC trading of listed securities. The fourth market is direct trading between institutions, usually through electronic networks."},
{"id":233,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A firm sells stock to a customer from its own inventory. The firm is acting as a:","c":["Dealer, and it charges a markup","Broker, and it charges a commission","Broker, and it charges a markup","Dealer, and it charges both a markup and a commission"],"e":"A firm trading for its own account acts as a dealer (principal) and earns a markup or markdown. A broker (agent) earns a commission. A firm cannot do both on the same trade."},
{"id":234,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"FINRA's 5% markup policy applies to:","c":["A riskless principal transaction in an over-the-counter stock","Shares of an IPO sold at the public offering price","Mutual fund shares","Municipal securities"],"e":"The 5% policy is a guideline for secondary market trades in non-exempt securities. It does not apply to securities sold with a prospectus at a fixed price, or to municipal securities."},
{"id":235,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A customer sells one stock and uses the proceeds to buy another on the same day. Under the 5% policy, the firm should:","c":["Calculate its compensation as if the customer had bought the second stock for cash","Charge a full markdown on the sale and a full markup on the purchase","Charge no compensation on either trade","Charge a commission only on the larger trade"],"e":"In a proceeds transaction, the total of the markdown on the sale and the markup on the purchase is measured against the amount invested in the purchase."},
{"id":236,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A market-wide circuit breaker halts trading for the rest of the day when the S&P 500 falls by:","c":["20%","7%","13%","10%"],"e":"Level 1 (7%) and Level 2 (13%) declines cause 15-minute halts if they occur before 3:25 p.m. ET. A Level 3 decline of 20% closes the market for the day."},
{"id":237,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A representative learns that a customer is about to enter a very large buy order for a stock. The representative first buys calls on that stock for his own account. This is:","c":["Front-running","Churning","Painting the tape","Selling away"],"e":"Front-running is trading for one's own benefit ahead of a customer order that is likely to move the price."},
{"id":238,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"Two traders repeatedly buy and sell the same stock between themselves to create the appearance of heavy trading. This is:","c":["Painting the tape","Front-running","Interpositioning","Backing away"],"e":"Painting the tape (matched orders) is a form of market manipulation meant to mislead other investors about trading activity."},
{"id":239,"f":4,"t":"Orders & trading","q":"A customer's market order is executed at $25.10, but the firm mistakenly reports the price to the customer as $25.01. The customer:","c":["Must accept the actual execution price of $25.10","May insist on the reported price of $25.01","May cancel the trade","Is owed the difference by the representative"],"e":"An incorrect report does not change the trade. The actual execution price is binding, and the firm sends a corrected report."},
{"id":240,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"Regular-way settlement for corporate stocks, corporate bonds and municipal bonds is:","c":["One business day after the trade date","The same day as the trade","Two business days after the trade date","Three business days after the trade date"],"e":"Regular-way settlement for most securities is T+1. Cash settlement is same day."},
{"id":241,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A customer buys stock in a cash account on Monday. Under Regulation T, the customer's payment is due by:","c":["Thursday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Friday"],"e":"Regulation T requires payment within two business days after the regular-way settlement date. With T+1 settlement, that is three business days after the trade."},
{"id":242,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A trade made for cash settlement settles:","c":["On the same day as the trade","On the next business day","Two business days after the trade","On a date chosen by the seller"],"e":"Cash settlement means delivery and payment on the trade date. It is often used when a seller needs proceeds right away or to capture a dividend."},
{"id":243,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A customer buys stock in a cash account and sells it before paying for it, without having funds on deposit. As a result, the account is:","c":["Frozen for 90 days","Closed permanently","Converted to a margin account","Restricted to selling for 30 days"],"e":"This is freeriding. The account is frozen for 90 days, during which the customer must have funds on deposit before buying."},
{"id":244,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A customer cannot pay for a purchase by the Regulation T deadline for a legitimate reason. An extension may be requested by:","c":["The broker-dealer, from its designated examining authority","The customer, from the SEC","The registered representative, from the issuer","The customer, from the Federal Reserve Board"],"e":"The firm requests the extension from its designated examining authority, such as FINRA, on the customer's behalf."},
{"id":245,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A trade confirmation must be sent to the customer:","c":["At or before completion of the transaction","Within 5 business days after the trade","With the next monthly statement","Only if the customer asks for one"],"e":"Customer confirmations must be given or sent at or before completion of the transaction, which is the settlement date."},
{"id":246,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"The civil penalty for insider trading can be as much as:","c":["Three times the profit made or loss avoided","The amount of the profit made","A flat fine of $100,000","Ten times the profit made or loss avoided"],"e":"Under the Insider Trading and Securities Fraud Enforcement Act, the SEC can seek civil penalties of up to three times the profit gained or loss avoided, in addition to criminal penalties."},
{"id":247,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"An award made in a FINRA arbitration is:","c":["Final and binding on all parties","A recommendation the parties may reject","Subject to appeal to FINRA's Board of Governors","Binding on the firm but not on the customer"],"e":"Arbitration awards are final and binding, with very limited grounds for a court to overturn them. Mediation, in contrast, is non-binding."},
{"id":248,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A registered representative receives an email from a customer complaining about unauthorized trades. The representative should:","c":["Forward it promptly to a supervising principal","Reply with an apology and keep a copy in her own files","Call the customer and try to settle the matter personally","Delete it if the customer later withdraws the complaint"],"e":"A written complaint, including email, must be brought to a principal's attention. The firm keeps complaint records and reports them to FINRA."},
{"id":249,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A representative wants to sell interests in a friend's private company to her customers and will be paid for each sale. She must:","c":["Give her firm prior written notice and receive its written approval","Do nothing, because the investment is not offered by her firm","Tell her firm within 30 days after the first sale","Notify FINRA directly"],"e":"This is a private securities transaction, also called selling away. Prior written notice is always required, and when compensation is involved the firm must approve in writing and supervise the activity."},
{"id":250,"f":4,"t":"Settlement & rules","q":"A representative wants to share in the profits and losses of a customer's account. This is allowed only if:","c":["The firm and the customer approve in writing, and sharing is proportionate","The customer agrees verbally and the firm is notified afterward","The representative guarantees the customer against losses","The representative has discretionary authority over the account"],"e":"Sharing in a customer account requires written authorization from both the firm and the customer, and must be in proportion to the amount the representative contributed. Guaranteeing against loss is prohibited."}];
